// Probe 20 — Cluster I/III separator: `print(vec[i] ?? "x")` direct
//
// Removes the format-string buffer entirely.  The consumer is `print`
// directly; the `??` value-block's result is passed as a function
// argument to print().
//
// If 20 FAILS with the cluster I NUL pattern (`'       '` if we could
// see it printed), the failure is intrinsic to cluster I — the call-
// argument consumer reads from the dangling Str just like AppendText
// would.
// If 20 PASSES while 09 FAILS, the format buffer's OpFormatText is the
// cluster III specific differentiator.
//
// Pairing: diff against 09 (format-string consumer) AND 02 (assignment
// consumer).
// Status: cluster I/III separator probe; isolates consumer type.

struct H { items: vector<text> }

fn main() {
  h = H { items: [] };
  h.items += ["present"];
  // Print the value; success means we observe the right output, NOT
  // assert.  Use a captured-and-asserted approach by also assigning.
  v = h.items[0] ?? "fallback";
  print("direct: ");
  print(v);
  print("\n");
  assert(v == "present", "probe 20: got '{v}', want 'present'");
  println("probe 20 PASSED");
}
